  HOW WE USE THEM, AND WHY THAT MATTERS. All four are ORDINARY INSTALLED
  DEPENDENCIES — `myuri` is declared in python/pyproject.toml and
  `@jwriter20/myuri` in js/package.json, each brings its cursory port as its own
  dependency, and all are resolved by the user's own package manager at install
  time. None is vendored into this repository, copied into our sources, or
  bundled into a build artifact: the TypeScript build is `tsc`, which leaves
  `require('@jwriter20/myuri')` as a runtime import rather than inlining it, and
  `js/scripts/copy-python.mjs` copies only our own `python/` tree.

  That arrangement is the one the LGPL is written for, and it is what lets this
  product remain under its own licence. It also means a user may replace any of
  these libraries with a modified version of their own — reinstall the package,
  and the driver picks it up — which is the freedom section 4 of the LGPL requires be
  preserved. Do not vendor, inline, or bundle any of them without taking legal
  advice first; doing so is the change that would put this obligation somewhere
  other than where it now sits.

  We have not modified any of them.
